{"schema": "taxonomy_pipeline/pair-scores-1", "similarity_model": "BAAI/bge-base-en-v1.5@a5beb1e3e68b9ab74eb54cfd186867f64f240e1a", "entailment_model": "tasksource/ModernBERT-large-nli@ca476cb923a8637073d4ceb0f19f7fc236e260d4", "texts": ["Fake news", "Fake news is a subcategory of misinformation", "Identity", "reputation", "It is particularly pernicious in that it undermines the credibility of standard news outlets", "Fake news is a subcategory of misinformation-incorrect or misleading information about the state of the world.", "In evaluating prevalence of fake news, we advocate focusing on publishers rather than individual stories, because we view the defining element of fake news to be the intent and processes of the publisher.", "Internet platforms have become the most important enablers and primary conduits of fake news.", "Most notably, fake news outlets lack the news media's editorial norms and processes for ensuring the accuracy and credibility of information.", "Social media are far more important than mainstream media as conduits for fake news sites (6).", "The Internet not only provides a platform for publishing (mis)information, but is a networked medium that actively promotes its dissemination.", "There exists little research focused specifically on fake news and no comprehensive data collection system to provide a dynamic understanding of how these pervasive systems are evolving.", "We have retained it both because of its value as a distinct scientific construct, and because its political salience helps draw attention to an important subject.", "even if fake news does not alter most people's beliefs, it may nonetheless reinforce existing false beliefs, increase their salience, or shape the news agenda, with potentially harmful effects for society", "even if fake news does not alter most people's beliefs, it may nonetheless reinforce existing false beliefs, increase their salience, or shape the news agenda, with potentially harmful effects for society.", "This psychological bond is usually termed \"a sense of belonging\" and such expressions point to the close link established between each individual and the collective self, namely the nation", "A nation's identity is defined by the nation's people not by the branding strategist. Any change in a nation's identity is either accomplished or accompanied by fundamental changes in the country's political, economic and social systems.", "Identity is about self perception.", "Nation identity, to paraphrase Albert and Whetten's definition of organisational identity (1985), is the collective understanding by a nation's people of the features presumed to be central and relatively permanent, and that distinguish the nation from other nations.", "National identity embodies the characteristics of a nation that its people perceive to be central, distinctive, and enduring (CED) in a nation when past, present and future is taken into account.", "National identity embodies the characteristics of a nation that its people perceive to be central, distinctive, and enduring (CED) in a nation when past, present and future is taken into account. It refers to the essentially irrational psychological bond that binds fellow nationals together and which is supposed to constitute the essence of national identity.", "This psychological bond is usually termed \"a sense of belonging\" and such expressions point to the close link established between each individual and the collective self, namely the nation (Triandafyllidou, 1998).", "a sense of belonging", "It is important to distinguish nation brand identity with national identity as these are two related but totally different constructs.", "Nation branding", "nation brand identity", "nation's image", "organisational identity", "sense of belonging", "fabricated information that mimics the output of the news media in form, but not in organizational process or intent", "Additional research demonstrates that people prefer information that confirms their pre-existing attitudes (selective exposure)", "Currently, one of the primary interventions focused on stemming the flow of fake news is fact checking: the direct identification of content that is factually incorrect.", "Research on fluency-the ease of information recall-and familiarity bias in politics shows that people tend to remember information, or how they feel about it, while forgetting the context within which they encountered it.", "We define fake news to be fabricated information that mimics the output of the news media in form, but not in organizational process or intent.", "automated accounts impersonating humans", "changing what we think about", "cyborgs (humans aided by automated posting to their accounts)", "humans aided by automated posting to their accounts", "incorrect or misleading information about the state of the world", "incorrect or misleading information about the state of the world.", "people prefer information that confirms their pre-existing attitudes", "people prefer information that confirms their pre-existing attitudes (selective exposure) and view information consistent with their pre-existing beliefs as more persuasive than dissonant information (confirmation bias)", "people tend to remember information, or how they feel about it, while forgetting the context within which they encountered it. They are also more likely to accept familiar information as true.", "social bots (automated accounts impersonating humans)", "the direct identification of content that is factually incorrect", "the direct identification of content that is factually incorrect.", "the ease of information recall", "view information consistent with their pre-existing beliefs as more persuasive than dissonant information", "fake news outlets lack the news media's editorial norms and processes for ensuring the accuracy and credibility of information", "It is particularly pernicious in that it undermines the credibility of standard news outlets.", "mental associations generated by knowledge and past experience.", "A nation's image is defined by the people outside the country; their perceptions are influenced by stereotyping, media coverage as well as personal experience.", "a nation's image is what a nation's people want the world to understand is most central, enduring and distinctive about their nation", "misinformation", "more likely to accept familiar information as true", "people tend to remember information, or how they feel about it, while forgetting the context within which they encountered it", "The literature presents contradictory evidence about the effectiveness of claim repetition in fact checking. Experimental and survey research has confirmed the basic familiarity effect, though recent experiments suggest that retractions are more effective in reducing misinformation effects when they explicitly repeat the misinformation.", "identity", "the characteristics of a nation that its people perceive to be central, distinctive, and enduring (CED) in a nation when past, present and future is taken into account.", "A brand, according to the classic definition by the American Marketing Association (1960), is a name, term, sign, symbol or design, or a combination of them which is intended to identify the goods or services of one seller or a group of sellers and to differentiate them from those of competitors.", "A more sophisticated definition is offered by Aaker (1996:68): a brand is a multidimensional assortment of functional, emotional, relational and strategic elements that collectively generate a unique set of associations in the public mind.", "A nation brand is a complicated multi-facet construct that is dependent on the context such as other countries, special events or occasions.", "A nation brand is the total sum of all perceptions of a nation in the mind of international stakeholders which may contain some of the following elements: people, place, culture/language, history, food, fashion, famous faces (celebrities), global brands etc.", "A nation's identity refers to the essentially irrational psychological bond that binds fellow nationals together and which is supposed to constitute the essence of national identity.", "Anholt (2007) has recently re-brand the concept as \"competitive identity\", which is described as a new model for enhancing national competitiveness using both public diplomacy and brand management.", "Image is what is projected to other", "Image is what is projected to other while reputation is the feedback received from other (Whetten and Mackey, 2002:400).", "In contrast with a global brand, a national brand is originated from a country, and its use is probably still confined to the country; while a global brand is recognised in the world marketplace.", "It refers to the essentially irrational psychological bond that binds fellow nationals together and which is supposed to constitute the essence of national identity.", "Nation branding is a process by which a nation's images can be created, monitored, evaluated and proactively managed in order to improve or enhance the country's reputation among a target international audience.", "National brand identity, on the other hand, refers to the identity of a specific \"nation brand\", not the nation. It is a set of associations the brand strategist seeks to create or maintain", "National brand identity, on the other hand, refers to the identity of a specific \"nation brand\", not the nation. It is a set of associations the brand strategist seeks to create or maintain (Aaker and Joachimsthaler, 2002:43).", "The way for a nation to gain a better reputation is to communicate to the international audience that how good you are, -this practice is called nation branding.", "Thus, a nation's image is what a nation's people want the world to understand is most central, enduring and distinctive about their nation", "Unlike the studies on COO and place branding which have a clear focus on promoting specific economic interests (export, tourism or inward investment), nation branding is concerned with a country's whole image on the international stage covering political, economic and cultural dimensions (Quelch and Jocz, 2004;Fan, 2006).", "a multidimensional assortment of functional, emotional, relational and strategic elements that collectively generate a unique set of associations in the public mind.", "a name, term, sign, symbol or design, or a combination of them which is intended to identify the goods or services of one seller or a group of sellers and to differentiate them from those of competitors.", "a new model for enhancing national competitiveness using both public diplomacy and brand management.", "a particular type of feedback received by the nation from the outside world, concerning the credibility of the nation's identity claims.", "a process by which a nation's images can be created, monitored, evaluated and proactively managed in order to improve or enhance the country's reputation among a target international audience.", "a set of associations the brand strategist seeks to create or maintain", "originated from a country, and its use is probably still confined to the country", "recognised in the world marketplace", "reputation is a particular type of feedback received by the nation from the outside world, concerning the credibility of the nation's identity claims", "reputation is a particular type of feedback received by the nation from the outside world, concerning the credibility of the nation's identity claims.", "reputation is the feedback received from other", "the collective understanding by a nation's people of the features presumed to be central and relatively permanent, and that distinguish the nation from other nations.", "the essentially irrational psychological bond that binds fellow nationals together and which is supposed to constitute the essence of national identity.", "the feedback received from other", "the identity of a specific \"nation brand\", not the nation.", "the identity of a specific \"nation brand\", not the nation. It is a set of associations the brand strategist seeks to create or maintain", "the total sum of all perceptions of a nation in the mind of international stakeholders which may contain some of the following elements: people, place, culture/language, history, food, fashion, famous faces (celebrities), global brands etc.", "what is projected to other"], "similarity": [[0, 1, 0.8658496737480164], [2, 3, 0.6432203054428101], [4, 5, 0.6731637716293335], [4, 6, 0.6345275640487671], [4, 7, 0.6152529120445251], [4, 8, 0.7323827147483826], [4, 9, 0.6334680914878845], [4, 10, 0.4984091818332672], [4, 11, 0.5718016624450684], [4, 12, 0.5245763659477234], [4, 13, 0.6617739796638489], [4, 14, 0.658632755279541], [15, 16, 0.6325582265853882], [15, 17, 0.6589117050170898], [15, 18, 0.6570706367492676], [15, 19, 0.6449381709098816], [15, 20, 0.7008261680603027], [15, 21, 0.9586396217346191], [22, 23, 0.5800637602806091], [22, 24, 0.5648723840713501], [22, 25, 0.5733404159545898], [22, 26, 0.6020936965942383], [22, 27, 0.6213191747665405], [22, 28, 0.9633651971817017], [29, 30, 0.5831589698791504], [29, 31, 0.7006114721298218], [29, 5, 0.7689802050590515], [29, 32, 0.5813198089599609], [29, 33, 0.8761152625083923], [29, 34, 0.6160213947296143], [29, 35, 0.5084724426269531], [29, 36, 0.5901591181755066], [29, 37, 0.5985256433486938], [29, 38, 0.666300892829895], [29, 39, 0.684601902961731], [29, 40, 0.6270726919174194], [29, 41, 0.6017090082168579], [29, 42, 0.5973817706108093], [29, 43, 0.6014667749404907], [29, 44, 0.7394644618034363], [29, 45, 0.7567164301872253], [29, 46, 0.5624048709869385], [29, 47, 0.683833122253418], [48, 5, 0.7525214552879333], [48, 6, 0.7194662094116211], [48, 7, 0.735632061958313], [48, 49, 0.7124930620193481], [48, 8, 0.8584544062614441], [48, 9, 0.752540647983551], [48, 10, 0.6085572242736816], [48, 11, 0.6396057605743408], [48, 12, 0.4793890416622162], [48, 13, 0.743934273